ggml-cpu: skip the barrier between graph nodes that cannot see each other's work

Every computed node ends with a barrier. Ling-3.0 computes 1,578 nodes per decoded token (MiniCPM5: 719), most of
them tiny ops. Every thread takes the same decision, from the graph alone:
- tiny simple nodes (at most 4,096 output values) run on thread 0 alone (nth = 1); the whitelisted ops split by
  rows or elements without cross-thread reductions, so results do not change;
- thread-0 chain: when the nodes since the last barrier and the next node all run on thread 0 only (tiny, or a
  row-split op on one row), no barrier is needed;
- independence: the next node and the nodes since the last barrier are simple ops (static ith/nth split, no shared
  wdata, chunk counter or internal barrier, plain types) with disjoint memory ranges.
A node that starts a fused group (RMS_NORM+MUL, the ADD chain) runs on all threads, so it never takes part; an
earlier version missed this and raced in Ling decode (KL 0.15). The abort callback is checked only at a kept
barrier, so every thread sees the flag at the same node. Ops with per-thread wdata (rope, soft_max,
set_rows, cpy to quantized) are excluded: their per-thread offsets depend on the op.
Barriers per decoded token: Ling 1,577 -> 903, MiniCPM5 718 -> 716. Results unchanged: x86 KL against stock at the
floor for decode at 3, 4 and 6 threads and for 512-token prefill. x86 decode (4 threads): Ling -1.8% at depth 0,
-0.9% at 1100 (an x86 barrier costs about 0.7 us, so running tiny ops on one thread does not pay there). Phone: not
measured yet; it decides.
